well let's find out how businesses in  northern ireland are faring  we can talk to patricia gilbert from the  bali lagan organic farm in bali claire  in county antrim uh thank you for being  with us we're looking at the politics of  all this but  what about the practicalities how are  you being  impacted by the checks and the  restrictions  well we run an organic farm we also have  a  farm shop and up until this year we  relied  very heavily on gb wholesalers for about  70 of our turnover we're now  in february and some of these  wholesalers say that they're still  unable to send produce across the rsc  right so you say unable to is that  because  they don't want to because of all the  extra  paperwork is is and possible delays i  guess to their produce  yes indeed i mean it's the amount of  paperwork that's required  the cost in terms of staff time  to fill everything in and then of course  the concern that produce particularly  fresh produce might get held up with the  ports  and so what are the particular  difficulties they're they're worried  about as a result of the new  restrictions is it is it simply the  paperwork you describe  or are there additional costs what are  the  the specific problems well certainly  there are additional costs and i'm  having to pay more  to get produce in from gb  but if i can give you one example we are  getting some organic fruit and veg from  a wholesaler in england  and they have to supply the courier  with all the information that's required  for the customs  documents they also have to upload  a full list of all the produce onto  the defrost website for a fatal sanitary  certificate which actually involves a  desperate  person having to come out and look at  the pallet of fruit and veg  and then come april  they will also have to get a certificate  of inspection because the goods they are  moving  are organic so that would be the third  level of inspection  and certificates right i mean boris  johnson  suggested that these could be teething  problems um  and also suggested that northern ireland  businesses could  have the best of both worlds um how is  it it's not turning out that way i guess  no and i i've spoken to a lot of other  businesses and it's not  actually just northern ireland because  the south of ireland is  as retailers are very effective as well  because  we all relied um on gb  wholesalers to a large extent  particularly for organic produce  so are you um having to source goods and  produce now from the eu are you looking  at other countries that would be  you know be simpler to deal with  well i have just placed my first order  with a large  dutch organic wholesaler um so we shall  see how that works  um but yes i mean if you know i didn't  really want to have to do that but i'm  i'm forced  down that road right so what would you  like to see happen  as talks going on i mean do you think  this this can be  resolved would you like to see a an  extension to the grace period would that  be enough  no i i don't think it's just about  extending the grace period i think  it has to be fixed um if  these regulations are in place to stop  the movement  of goods from northern ireland across  the border  downside in other words as a back door  to eu and i think they have to look at  where  the actual risk is and  where these goods are actually arriving  in northern ireland and  i'm a retailer i'm bringing in pallets  of maybe one to two hundred lines i'm  going to sell it out of  a farm shop there isn't no risk that i  am going to move those goods  across the border into the south of  ireland and i think there has to be a  much more pragmatic approach to this  okay well we'll see what happens  patricia gilbert uh thank you for  uh being with us this evening
 

